# Hook authoring

Guards fail when they match a guessed payload and never observe a live
denial. Write matchers against the shapes below, then prove the hook with a
spawn-level test and one live block. See
[`.cursor/hooks/linear-spawn-guard.mjs`](.cursor/hooks/linear-spawn-guard.mjs),
[`.cursor/hooks/tdd-delegation-guard.mjs`](.cursor/hooks/tdd-delegation-guard.mjs),
and [`.cursor/hooks/git-stage-guard.mjs`](.cursor/hooks/git-stage-guard.mjs).

## Observed payload shapes

`preToolUse` stdin is a JSON object with `tool_name`, `tool_input` (object),
`hook_event_name`, `cwd`, plus session metadata. `tool_name` is the Cursor
tool (`Shell`, `Write`, `Task`, …), not an MCP tool.

`beforeMCPExecution` stdin is a JSON object where `tool_name` is the MCP
tool (`get_issue`, `get_project`, …), `tool_input` is a **JSON string**, and
`mcp_server_name` (also seen as `command` or `url`) is the server key
(`plugin-linear-linear`, `plugin-vercel-vercel`, …).

`StrReplace` arrives as `tool_name: "Write"` with the target at
`tool_input.path`. A matcher on `"StrReplace"` never fires.

MCP calls never arrive as `preToolUse` `CallMcpTool`. A matcher on that
name never fires. Shared parsing lives in
[`extractMcpCall`](.cursor/hooks/lib/mcp-payload.mjs) — do not
re-guess the shape in a new hook.

`readStdinJson` must strip a leading BOM. Cursor prefixes hook stdin with
one on Windows; `JSON.parse` rejects it.

## Liveness standard

A detector unit test plus a [`hooks.json`](.cursor/hooks.json) wiring
assertion are not proof. A guard is not done until it has:

1. a spawn-level test that feeds the **real** payload shape (including the
   BOM) through the hook script, and
2. one observed live denial of the condition it claims to block.

Follow the restore pattern in
[`.cursor/checks/tdd-guard-policy.test.mjs`](.cursor/checks/tdd-guard-policy.test.mjs):
capture the state file once at import, restore in a single `after()`, run
the spawn block at `{ concurrency: 1 }`. A per-test capture can leak an
armed state.

## Probe safety

Never install an unconditional deny in a hook without a matcher. A
failClosed hook that denies every tool blocks the revert that would remove
it. Trigger the guard's real condition instead (a scratch `Write` under a
protected prefix, a non-whitelisted `Shell` command) and prefer probes that
log without denying.

## Armed-window safety

An armed guard must never deny its own disarm. Every armed window needs an
escape that does not route through the guarded tool — for the TDD guard,
`Write` can set
[`.cursor/hooks/state/tdd-guard.json`](.cursor/hooks/state/tdd-guard.json)
to `{"armed": false}` without going through `Shell`.

## beforeMCPExecution does not hot-reload mid-session

Observed while adding `linear-comment-size-guard.mjs`: a newly-added
`preToolUse` entry fired correctly on the very next matching call in the same
session (proven by `task-delegation-guard.mjs` denying a live Task
immediately after being wired into [`hooks.json`](.cursor/hooks.json)), but a
newly-added `beforeMCPExecution` entry did not fire on five separate live
`save_comment` calls in that same session — including after reordering the
array and waiting — despite an already-registered `beforeMCPExecution` hook
(`linear-spawn-guard.mjs`) firing correctly throughout. The detector and
wiring were independently confirmed correct by a spawn-level test feeding
the real payload shape.

Conclusion: `beforeMCPExecution` hook registrations appear to load once per
session and do not pick up new `hooks.json` entries until the window is
reloaded, even though `preToolUse` does. Do not conclude a
`beforeMCPExecution` guard is broken from a same-session live-fire failure
alone — the spawn-level test is authoritative for logic; the live denial for
a *new* `beforeMCPExecution` hook needs a reload of the Cursor window (or a
fresh session) before it can be observed.
